CLASS: Ed25519KeyPair (lines 16–83)
====================================================================

Purpose: Ed25519 cryptographic key pair management

Attributes:
  - private_key: ed25519.Ed25519PrivateKey instance
  - public_key: ed25519.Ed25519PublicKey instance
  - private_key_bytes: 32-byte raw private key seed
  - public_key_bytes: 32-byte raw public key

Key Methods:

  sign(message: bytes) -> bytes
    Signature: Ed25519(message, private_key)
    Output: 64-byte signature
    Deterministic: Same input → same signature

  verify(message: bytes, signature: bytes) -> bool
    Verification: Ed25519.verify(signature, message, public_key)
    Returns: True if valid, False otherwise
    Security: Constant-time comparison (via cryptography library)

  get_public_key_pem() -> str
    Export: PEM-encoded public key for distribution
    Format: PKCS#8 SubjectPublicKeyInfo
    Use case: Key distribution to other capsules/agents

====================================================================
CLASS: CapsuleEngine (lines 85–249)
====================================================================

Purpose: Core cryptographic integrity engine for CAPSULE

State Variables:
  - capsule_id: str (e.g., "capsule-001")
  - state: Dict (mutable capsule state)
  - merkle_root: Optional[str] (current Merkle root, None initially)
  - keypair: Ed25519KeyPair (signing key material)
  - audit_trail: List[Dict] (immutable operation log)
  - _mutation_counter: int (replay attack prevention)

====================================================================
METHOD: mutate(operation: str, data: Dict) -> str
====================================================================

Purpose: Apply state mutation and update Merkle root

Algorithm:
  1. Increment _mutation_counter (prevents replay attacks)
  2. Create payload: "{operation}:{data}:{counter}".encode()
  3. Sign payload: signature = Ed25519.sign(payload)
  4. Update state:
     - If operation == "SET": merge data into state
     - If operation == "DELETE": remove keys from state
  5. Compute new Merkle root:
     - old_root_bytes = bytes.fromhex(merkle_root) if merkle_root else None
     - merkle_root = _compute_merkle_root(old_root_bytes, payload, signature)
  6. Append to audit trail:
     {
       "operation": operation,
       "capsule_id": capsule_id,
       "timestamp": datetime.utcnow().isoformat(),
       "signature": signature.hex(),
       "merkle_hash": merkle_root,
       "payload": payload.hex()
     }
  7. Return merkle_root

Security Properties:
  ✓ Non-repudiation: Each mutation is signed
  ✓ Immutability: Audit trail cannot be modified without invalidating Merkle root
  ✓ Monotonicity: Counter prevents same mutation being applied twice

====================================================================
METHOD: _compute_merkle_root(old_root, payload, signature) -> str
====================================================================

Cryptographic Formula:

  inner_hash = SHA256(payload || signature)
  merkle_root = SHA256(old_root || inner_hash)

Specification:
  - old_root: Previous Merkle root bytes (None for first mutation)
    Treatment: None → empty bytes ("")
  - payload: UTF-8 encoded mutation payload
  - signature: 64-byte Ed25519 signature
  - Output: 64-character hex string (SHA256 digest)

Example Trace:

  Mutation 1:
    payload = "SET:{'key': 'value'}:1"
    signature = Ed25519.sign(payload, privkey)
    inner = SHA256(payload || signature)
    merkle_root_1 = SHA256("" || inner) = HASH_1

  Mutation 2:
    payload = "SET:{'key2': 'value2'}:2"
    signature = Ed25519.sign(payload, privkey)
    inner = SHA256(payload || signature)
    merkle_root_2 = SHA256(HASH_1 || inner) = HASH_2

  Mutation 3:
    payload = "DELETE:{'key'}:3"
    signature = Ed25519.sign(payload, privkey)
    inner = SHA256(payload || signature)
    merkle_root_3 = SHA256(HASH_2 || inner) = HASH_3

Property: Each root depends on all previous mutations (path-dependent)

====================================================================
METHOD: verify_merkle_root() -> bool
====================================================================

Purpose: Detect tampering by reconstructing Merkle root from audit trail

Algorithm:
  1. If audit_trail is empty, return (merkle_root is None)
  2. For each entry in audit_trail:
     a. Extract: payload_hex, signature_hex, (skip merkle_hash)
     b. Reconstruct: computed_root = _compute_merkle_root(old, payload, sig)
     c. Update: old = computed_root
  3. Return: computed_root == self.merkle_root

Tamper Scenarios Detected:

  ✓ Merkle root altered:
    - computed_root will not match corrupted merkle_root
    - Returns False

  ✓ Audit trail modified:
    - Any payload change produces different hash
    - Cascade propagates to final root
    - Returns False

  ✓ Audit trail entry deleted:
    - Chain breaks; reconstruction diverges
    - Returns False

  ✓ Audit trail reordered:
    - Different sequence of hashes
    - Returns False (unless attacker recomputes entire chain)

Security Model: "Append-only ledger with cryptographic integrity"

====================================================================
METHOD: verify_signature(payload: str, signature_hex: str) -> bool
====================================================================

Purpose: Verify Ed25519 signature for a given payload

Process:
  1. Decode signature: bytes.fromhex(signature_hex)
  2. Verify: keypair.verify(payload.encode(), signature)
  3. Return: True if valid, False otherwise

Use case: Peer verification (third party verifies capsule's signature)

====================================================================
SECURITY ANALYSIS
====================================================================

Threat Model: Integrity & Non-Repudiation

Attack Vector: Mutation Tampering
  - Attacker: Modifies state or audit trail
  - Detection: verify_merkle_root() fails
  - Mitigation: Ed25519 signature prevents forgery

Attack Vector: Replay Attack
  - Attacker: Re-applies past mutation
  - Detection: _mutation_counter prevents duplicates
  - Mitigation: Each mutation has unique counter

Attack Vector: Out-of-Order Mutation
  - Attacker: Reorders audit trail entries
  - Detection: Merkle chain breaks
  - Mitigation: Path-dependent hashing

Attack Vector: Key Theft
  - Assumption: Private key not exposed
  - If exposed: Attacker can forge signatures
  - Mitigation: Tier 2+ will add multi-signature

Known Limitations (Tier 1):
  ✗ No Byzantine fault tolerance
  ✗ No multi-signature consensus
  ✗ No post-quantum crypto
  ✗ No distributed trust

